lying topoi)\nand we are looking to find a homomorphism of sheaves of rings\n<MATH_0020> fitting into it.\n\n<STRUCT_0003><STRUCT_0004>\nIn the rest of the proof of the lemma we assume\nall underlying topological spaces are the\nsame, i.e., we have a diagram (<REF_0005>)\nof sheaves on a site <MATH_0021> (with <MATH_0022>\non underlying topoi) and we are looking for\nhomomorphisms of sheaves of rings\n<MATH_0023> fitting into it.\nAs ext groups we will use\n<MATH_0024>, <MATH_0025>.\n\n<STRUCT_0005><STRUCT_0006>\nStep 1. Construction of the obstruction class. Consider the sheaf\nof sets\n<MATH_0026>\nThis comes with a surjective map <MATH_0027>\nand hence we can use <MATH_0028> instead of\n<MATH_0029>, see\nModules on Sites, Lemma <REF_0006>.\nSet\n<MATHSEG_0001>and<MATHSEG_0002>\nThere is a surjection <MATH_0030> whose kernel\nis <MATH_0031>.\nWe obtain two homomorphisms of <MATH_0032>-algebras\n<MATHSEG_0003>and<MATHSEG_0004>\nwhich induce maps <MATH_0033> and\n<MATH_0034>. Both <MATH_0035> and <MATH_0036>\nannihilate <MATH_0037>. Moreover <MATH_0038> and <MATH_0039> agree on\n<MATH_0040>\nas maps into <MATH_0041>\nbecause the left hand square of (<REF_0007>)\nis commutative. Thus the difference\n<MATH_0042>\ninduces a well defined <MATH_0043>-linear map\n<MATH_0044>\nwhich sends the class of a local section <MATH_0045> of <MATH_0046> to\n<MATH_0047> where <MATH_0048> is a lift of <MATH_0049> to a local\nsection of <MATH_0050>. We let\n<MATH_0051>\nbe the image (see below).\n\n<STRUCT_0007><STRUCT_0008>\nStep 2. Vanishing of <MATH_0052> is necessary. Let us write <MATH_0053>.\nObserve that <MATH_0054>\nfits into a distinguished triangle\n<MATH_0055>\nThus we see that <MATH_0056> is zero if and only if <MATH_0057>\nis a composition <MATH_0058>\nfor some map <MATH_0059>. Suppose there exists a\nhomomorphisms of sheaves of rings\n<MATH_0060> fitting into\n(<REF_0008>). In this case consider the map\n<MATH_0061>,\n<MATH_0062>. A calculation\nshows this annihilates <MATH_0063>\nand induces a derivation\n<MATH_0064>.\nThe resulting linear map <MATH_0065> witnesses the\nfact that <MATH_0066> in this case.\n\n<STRUCT_0009><STRUCT_0010>\nStep 3. Vanishing of <MATH_0067> is sufficient. Let\n<MATH_0068> be a <MATH_0069>-linear map\nsuch that <MATH_0070> is equal to\n<MATH_0071>.\nThen a calculation shows that\n<MATH_0072>\nannihilates <MATH_0073> and hence defines a map\n<MATH_0074> fitting into\n(<REF_0009>).\n\n<STRUCT_0011><STRUCT_0012>\nProof of (2) in the special case above. Omitted. Hint:\nThis is exactly the same as the proof of (2) of\nLemma <REF_0010>.\n", "source_text_hash": "sha256:198ba44b3b68c46a9b6b783f7cba121f003bb0cd206fe84a22c191c4ee87a9e8", "unit_id": "tag:08UG:proof"}
